meta {
  name: Forgot Password (Public)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/forgot-password
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{producerEmail}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 202 Accepted", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(202);
  });

  test("says nothing about whether the account exists", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().message).to.equal(
      "If an account exists for that address, a password reset link is on its way."
    );
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /auth/forgot-password — self-service reset request (PAC-81)

  Public. The "Forgot password?" entry point on the login page. Reuses the
  PAC-79 reset-token plumbing — same digest, same expiry, same email, same
  completion endpoints (`GET /auth/password-reset/:token`,
  `POST /auth/reset-password`).

  **Module:** none. **Auth:** none.
  **Throttle:** `PASSWORD_RESET_REQUEST_RATE_LIMIT` (5/min) +
  `PASSWORD_RESET_REQUEST_HOURLY_LIMIT` (15/h) per IP.

  **Body**
  | field | type   | rules       |
  |-------|--------|-------------|
  | email | string | valid email |

  ## The response is always the same `202`

  Unknown address, deactivated user, pending invite, platform admin, an
  address belonging to a different host, or a request inside the per-user
  resend cooldown (60s) — every one of them returns the identical body below,
  and nothing else. Anything more specific would make an unauthenticated
  route an account oracle. A link is actually mailed only when the address
  belongs to an **active agency user who could sign in on the requesting
  host** (mirrors `assertBelongsOnHost`).

  There is deliberately no dev-mode `resetToken` in this response (unlike the
  admin-triggered `POST /users/:userId/password-reset`) — that would break
  the uniform-response property. In dev, read the link from the worker's
  email log or the Inngest event.

  **Response `202`**
  ```json
  { "message": "If an account exists for that address, a password reset link is on its way." }
  ```

  **Errors** — `400` malformed email · `429` rate limit. Nothing else.
}
